1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,980
It's day one of a healthier, smarter, better you.
这是更健康、更聪明、更优秀的你的第一天。

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:06,340
You're full of zest and good intentions.
你充满热情，目的明确。

3
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:14,390
You woke up at 5am to run before work, you read a book in your lunchbreak, and you listened to an English podcast on your commute.
你早上5点起床，在工作前跑步，午休时读书，通勤路上听英语播客。

4
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:17,190
Now, fast forward three months.
现在，快进到三个月后。

5
00:00:17,190 --> 00:00:24,720
Life is getting in the way, and you've slipped into old habits… snoozing your alarm and eating chocolate for breakfast.
生活无法正常进行，你又陷入了旧习惯……按掉闹钟，早餐吃巧克力。

6
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:26,450
Sound familiar?
听起来耳熟吗？

7
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:28,710
Don't be hard on yourself.
不要对自己太苛刻。

8
00:00:28,710 --> 00:00:31,710
It's unrealistic to rely on willpower alone.
单靠意志力是不现实的。

9
00:00:31,710 --> 00:00:37,390
As James Clear, author of the bestselling book 'Atomic Habits', writes, "The problem isn't you.
正如畅销书《原子习惯》的作者詹姆斯·克利尔所写："问题不在于你。

10
00:00:37,390 --> 00:00:40,000
The problem is your system."
问题在于你的系统。"

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,840
Our habits come from goals, so start by getting really clear.
我们的习惯源于目标，所以首先要弄清楚目标。

12
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:54,820
Goals, according to George T Doran, should be SMART: specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound.
根据乔治·T·多兰的说法，目标应该是SMART的：具体的、可衡量的、可实现的、相关的和有时限的。

13
00:00:54,820 --> 00:00:59,390
It's not enough to say, "I'll exercise more" – that's too vague!
只说"我要多锻炼"是不够的——那太模糊了！

14
00:00:59,390 --> 00:01:05,330
A SMART goal would be "I'm going to walk for 20 minutes in the evenings on weekdays."
一个SMART的目标应该是"我打算在工作日的晚上步行20分钟。"

15
00:01:05,330 --> 00:01:08,720
Now you have a clear action that you can stick to.
现在你有了一个可以坚持的明确行动。

16
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:15,360
Once you're clear on your goals, you need to make the associated habits as automatic as possible.
一旦你明确了目标，就需要让相关的习惯尽可能自动化。

17
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:25,140
Try 'habit stacking', a technique popularised in the book 'Atomic Habits', which involves attaching a new habit onto something you already do regularly.
尝试"习惯叠加"——这是《原子习惯》一书中推广的一种技巧，即把一个新习惯附加到你已经在做的某件事上。

18
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:31,310
For example, if you already make coffee every morning, use that moment to read your book for 20 minutes.
例如，如果你每天早上都煮咖啡，那就利用那个时刻读20分钟的书。

19
00:01:31,310 --> 00:01:36,930
That way, your new reading habit is now intertwined with something you're going to do anyway.
这样，你的新阅读习惯就与你本来就要做的事情交织在一起了。

20
00:01:36,930 --> 00:01:44,080
Another key strategy is to reward yourself, to transform the habit from a chore into something pleasurable.
另一个关键策略是奖励自己，把习惯从一件苦差事变成一件愉快的事情。

21
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:54,930
If it's hard to get yourself to the gym, promise yourself that after each visit, you'll allow yourself a treat – a takeaway meal or an episode of your favourite TV show.
如果很难让自己去健身房，那就向自己保证，每次去完之后，你会给自己一个奖励——一顿外卖或一集你最喜欢的电视节目。

22
00:01:54,930 --> 00:02:05,250
Even with our best intentions and a great system, stress and interruptions to your routine like social events can still make keeping habits feel like an uphill battle.
即使我们怀着最好的意愿和优秀的方式方法，压力和社交活动等对日常生活的干扰仍然会让保持习惯感觉像一场艰苦的战斗。

23
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:10,720
If or when you have setbacks, accept it as part of the process.
如果你或当你遇到挫折时，接受它作为过程的一部分。

24
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:16,550
Life is full of spontaneity and uncertainty – one could argue that's what keeps it interesting!
生活充满了自发性和不确定性——有人可能会说，这正是它有趣的地方！

25
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:20,200
Use setbacks as a pause for reflection and review.
把挫折当作暂停，以便反思和回顾。

26
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:22,490
What about your system is working?
你的习惯养成系统中哪些部分在起作用？

27
00:02:22,490 --> 00:02:24,540
What's no longer serving you?
哪些不再对你有用？

28
00:02:24,540 --> 00:02:26,760
Then, get back on track.
然后，重新回到正轨。
